<BaseLayout title="Data Methodology" description="How Nursing Career Data sources, validates, and presents U.S. nursing wage information.">
<main>
  <p class="eyebrow">Transparency</p>
  <h1>Data methodology</h1>
  <p class="lead">Our goal is to publish nursing wage information with clear definitions, source attribution, and reference dates.</p>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Primary data source</h2>
    <p>Nursing Career Data provides nursing salary and employment information based on official U.S. labor statistics. Our goal is to make occupational wage data easier to understand, compare, and explore across the United States.</p>
    <p>Our primary data source is the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS), Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS) program.</p>
    <p>This website uses the May 2025 OEWS data release for Registered Nurses (SOC 29-1141), including national and state-level occupational employment and wage estimates.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.bls.gov/oes/2025/may/">U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics – May 2025 OEWS Data →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Reference period</h2>
    <p>The salary estimates presented on this website refer to the May 2025 OEWS reference period.</p>
    <p>These figures represent occupational wage estimates for the specified reporting period. They should not be interpreted as real-time salary figures or guaranteed earnings for individual nurses.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Understanding wage measures</h2>
    <p>BLS OEWS provides several different wage measures:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Annual mean wage:</strong> The average annual wage across workers in an occupation.</li>
      <li><strong>Annual median wage:</strong> The midpoint of the wage distribution.</li>
      <li><strong>Hourly mean wage:</strong> The average hourly wage.</li>
      <li><strong>Employment estimates:</strong> The estimated number of people employed in an occupation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These measures describe different aspects of the labor market and should not be treated as interchangeable.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>State-level comparisons</h2>
    <p>State-level salary pages use BLS OEWS occupational estimates to help readers compare Registered Nurse wages across different U.S. states.</p>
    <p>Differences in reported wages may reflect regional labor markets, employer characteristics, industry composition, and local economic conditions.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Data limitations</h2>
    <p>BLS OEWS estimates are statistical estimates, not exact measurements of every individual worker's earnings.</p>
    <p>Actual nursing salaries may vary depending on experience, education, specialization, employer, location, shift differentials, overtime, and other factors.</p>
    <p>The estimates should be used as a general reference for occupational wage comparisons rather than as a guarantee of individual compensation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Data updates</h2>
    <p>We aim to review and update salary information when new BLS OEWS releases become available.</p>
    <p>When updated data are incorporated, the reference period will be identified so readers can understand which reporting year the figures represent.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Transparency and accuracy</h2>
    <p>Our commitment is to present nursing salary information with clear source attribution, consistent definitions, and transparent reference periods.</p>
    <p>We do not present unverified salary figures as official BLS estimates.</p>
  </section>
</main>
</BaseLayout>
